<PageLayout title="Editorial Policy: How We Research and Update Guides" heading="Editorial policy" description="How DistritoGamer researches, writes, reviews, updates and corrects gaming guides, and keeps ads and affiliate links separate from editorial decisions." path="/editorial-policy/" updated="2026-10-06"
  intro="The standards every guide on DistritoGamer is written to, and what you can hold us to.">
  <h2>Our purpose</h2>
  <p>We publish guides to help players improve, configure their setups and solve problems. A guide is worth publishing only if it would help someone who already read the top results for the same question. We would rather publish fewer, better guides than hundreds of thin ones.</p>
  <h2>How a guide is made</h2>
  <ol>
    <li><strong>Question first.</strong> We start from a specific problem a player has, such as high input lag or an unreliable aim routine, and decide what a complete answer needs to include.</li>
    <li><strong>Research.</strong> We check how the underlying system works, read vendor and developer documentation, and consult reputable references. Sources worth citing are linked in the guide.</li>
    <li><strong>Original writing.</strong> We write every guide ourselves. We do not copy, spin or lightly reword other sites, and we do not publish text generated by others as our own research.</li>
    <li><strong>Review.</strong> A second pass checks accuracy, clarity, safety of any system change we suggest, and whether advice is reversible.</li>
    <li><strong>Publication.</strong> Each guide shows an author, a published date and an updated date.</li>
  </ol>
  <h2>Testing claims</h2>
  <p>We never present a product as personally tested unless it was. We do not fabricate prices, ratings, specifications, benchmarks or user reviews. Where a guide describes a category of gear without testing, we describe what to look for and who it suits instead of ranking models.</p>
  <h2>Updates and corrections</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Evergreen guides</strong> are reviewed every 6 to 12 months.</li>
    <li><strong>Game-specific guides</strong> are reviewed after major patches or when readers report that advice no longer applies.</li>
    <li>The &ldquo;updated&rdquo; date changes only for substantive revisions, never for cosmetic edits.</li>
    <li>If we get something wrong, we correct it in place and, for significant errors, add a note describing what changed.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>To report an error, use our <a href="/contact/">contact page</a>.</p>
  <h2>Advertising, affiliate links and independence</h2>
  <p>Advertising and affiliate relationships are separate from editorial decisions. Advertisers and merchants do not see or approve guides before publication, and they cannot buy a recommendation. Affiliate links are used only where a product recommendation helps the reader, are marked as such, and are explained in our <a href="/affiliate-disclosure/">affiliate disclosure</a>. Sponsored content, if we ever publish it, will be labeled clearly.</p>
  <h2>Safety of system changes</h2>
  <p>Many guides suggest changing settings on your PC or console. We prefer reversible changes, tell you what to note down before changing something, and avoid tweaks that disable security protections or void warranties. If a change carries a meaningful risk, the guide says so.</p>
  <h2>Imagery</h2>
  <p>Cover images are original illustrations made for the site or properly licensed photographs, credited where required. We do not use artificially violent or disturbing imagery.</p>
  <h2>Comments or concerns</h2>
  <p>If you believe a guide breaks these standards, tell us through the <a href="/contact/">contact page</a>. We take it seriously.</p>
</PageLayout>
